#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180479} :activity/id #uuid "545f2edf-8d2f-583e-8ce1-05c1095b6170" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687 :topic/id #uuid "5f0fbf1a-01fe-5c95-8b44-55eb40e24c08" :topic/math-academy-id 3708} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180713 :tutorial/content "The line integral with respect to $x$ of the function $f(x,y)$ along the path $C$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf r (t)$ for $a\\leq t\\leq b$ is a parametrization of $C.$ \n\nNow, if we set $f(x,y) = 1,$ then by the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the $x$-coordinates at the initial and terminal points of $C,$ respectively. To summarize,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x = x_2 - x_1.\n$$\n\nLet's use this result to calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d x,$ where $C$ is the path along the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, traversed in the clockwise direction, as shown below:\n\n![](images/01/01b5f21b2c778f62f7dd884065ee4ce17dd01bd021063fc7c788f09a89feea86.png)\n\nThe initial point is $(0,2)$ and the terminal point is $(2,0).$ So, we have $x_1 = 0, x_2 = 2,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x = 2-0 = 2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c57d3ca1-3c93-5d88-8e4f-5bee849ea14e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5814 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e23b5a77-ead0-581f-bcc1-2f32ba803361" :step/math-academy-id 35641 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179731 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179593 :question/id #uuid "30d54dcc-49a2-5cd0-9eb9-659413ed5c67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9525"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79e281dd-40c0-515a-a5d5-d063609ecf73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051000}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426489}] :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to X"} :step/id #uuid "ba17887b-5323-555c-9d19-93a2e99bcb3d" :step/math-academy-id 35654 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179732 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179594 :question/id #uuid "d799bcde-dbf0-580e-921b-18e56d729ea1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9526"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6935944-0acd-5593-aa80-e56f6014c13f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259680}] :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to Y"} :step/id #uuid "97614692-6b8b-51e8-88ea-05390514a3cc" :step/math-academy-id 35655 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179733 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179595 :question/id #uuid "e1d24f1c-4b7c-5c5e-bf93-7f9e76dffdf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fd19952-77af-5f27-a7ef-adf46b1958ae" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415678}] :knowledge-point/title "Orientation-Dependence"} :step/id #uuid "5792d95e-415c-55aa-9d58-b81c51e1404f" :step/math-academy-id 35660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180714 :tutorial/content "In addition, we note the following regarding line integrals with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, then\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  \\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n- When $C$ is a *closed* curve, we sometimes use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\n \nIf the curve is traversed once, the initial and terminal points are the same, so $x_1 = x_2.$ Therefore, for line integrals of unity with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\,\\textrm d x = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c1ae4ef-4256-598c-b44d-3421c8c4ebe1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5817 :tutorial/title "Some Remarks"} :step/id #uuid "b7182be2-ddc6-5d8c-9367-0bf313338059" :step/math-academy-id 35673 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180480}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180715 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\,\\textrm d x = 2,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the path along the quarter-circle shown below.\n\n![](images/01/01b5f21b2c778f62f7dd884065ee4ce17dd01bd021063fc7c788f09a89feea86.png)\n\nIf we reverse the orientation of the curve, we get the following path:\n\n![](images/46/469675e85a6ca1d23de6da985d36e8e86c859ceff38ea6849984ea3c2cd34abe.png)\n\nThe initial and terminal points have swapped, and so we now have $x_1 = 2$ and $x_2 = 0.$ Applying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-C}\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates an important property of line integrals with respect to $x,$ described in full as follows:\n\n*Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n*Similarly, *\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ are *dependent on orientation*. This is quite different from the case for line integrals with respect to arc length, which are independent of orientation." :tutorial/id #uuid "6fb52502-a498-5599-851c-64cf19d11bf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5818 :tutorial/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"} :step/id #uuid "fb92124a-a40c-5b95-8acb-21f039c39f10" :step/math-academy-id 35674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180716 :tutorial/content "Similar to line integrals of unity with respect to $x,$ the line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d y = y_2 - y_1,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_2$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and $y_1$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point. \n\nAlso, if $C$ is a closed, positively oriented curve traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\,\\textrm d y = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "16485104-9964-58c8-8611-2769190cbf9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5819 :tutorial/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to Y"} :step/id #uuid "e5542a0f-35f9-5d2c-a937-679f3e3b6ef6" :step/math-academy-id 35675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180717 :tutorial/content "![](images/c2/c2ab6be66d91822186ee94c846f8cd1a247da776b89d7d9220b836193b9b04d3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ above. Earlier, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\,\\textrm d x = 2,\n$$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- In the context of our usual interpretation of line integrals with respect to $x,$ this result represents the total area accumulated when the function $z=1$ is projected onto the plane $y=0$ as $C$ is traversed from the initial point $(0,2)$ to the terminal point $(2,0).$ \n\n![](images/29/297e4e302b0cec7f4448ec7e8b36ab6c1f84d79d5f71aad1c6887d86d54dc328.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that this projection generates a rectangle of length $x_2 - x_1 = 2$ and height $z=1.$ Since the integral of unity with respect to $x$ along $C$ is equal to the area of this rectangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x & =1\\cdot ({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, this result is *positive* because the rectangle is traced out in the direction of *increasing* $x.$\n\n- If we reverse the direction of the curve then we get a *negative* result. This is because the rectangle is traced out in the direction of *decreasing* $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/17/179713734445f85818ef4e46cfa5948115cea936538acd3095cbfa706d381972.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ba2d901-3b8c-5ce9-a7bd-ace7643f3277" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5820 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind Some of the Results"} :step/id #uuid "a896d261-9315-51e1-80bb-4191a37e68b3" :step/math-academy-id 35676}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179861}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426490} :activity/id #uuid "76e8a030-4b20-5150-8ff6-5733d6c02963" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687 :topic/id #uuid "5f0fbf1a-01fe-5c95-8b44-55eb40e24c08" :topic/math-academy-id 3708} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075ffae1-ea68-5f4d-bb63-f49b56d8e6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Impossible to determine" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256845} {:answer/id #uuid "51a75927-20c3-52d1-a64d-8ec72f7cabd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257476} {:answer/id #uuid "645886ef-4aa0-5f2b-bb93-dc06146a70f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257662} {:answer/id #uuid "7afaaf8e-21fa-551e-b15c-0317c5d6ef18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257872} {:answer/id #uuid "99fb9ef2-4ee0-53a2-a318-7d1eb71ac286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257476} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb49f773-46c7-535f-a9e1-dc1765ec0827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "42bfa60d-7b26-5dc6-9634-a2e58886024d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124179" :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=3$ and ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=5,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3+5 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "057ed5af-875f-53e1-addf-521f93615e7d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55c54a1b-25be-5636-b467-f5ac1a9b52a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257520} {:answer/id #uuid "90ca79dc-a39f-5a16-a0bb-06f5058a2325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258059} {:answer/id #uuid "90fced00-d179-5243-b71b-5f2af240f1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5+5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258062} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d9b801-9124-53be-9f21-3845b38cdb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258919} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d92c55-a2da-5101-b6a0-b111b36b6752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258919} :answer-field/id #uuid "4daa3094-63d7-51fd-af1c-c3ff26a2ec4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6bbf9141-617f-5455-b7c6-ab9b0397e741" :question/math-academy-id "q-124336" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbef822c90e494cbe75e4a0b4ef93b058fccdd0111ec997f13930b1dba44406d.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is the path comprising of two line segments, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{2}=0$ and ${y}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "494b3b4e-680e-5d29-ad8a-21380b15d664"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10710cf3-2461-52e0-8591-26504aac5b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426476} {:answer/id #uuid "268e558d-6b3a-5fd2-8942-b57b576a8436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426478} {:answer/id #uuid "2abac835-c02a-57a9-ab86-5e5d2da016b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426479} {:answer/id #uuid "40b22bf4-a6c7-569e-8fd1-56a445471c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426480} {:answer/id #uuid "70a9bb49-2c55-5047-8c58-bf5ec89dd003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426481} :answer-field/id #uuid "00fb8aa7-71b4-5be7-ac8f-e5b17414c65f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "15dd7515-4c6a-53a1-ac3c-ffc4846bc122" :question/math-academy-id "q-124328" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4d60c547afdb873c30e6c5e1f70d616dc31ace79b482f37ae51ea4f036d5a42.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the closed path along the quarter circle of \nradius $2,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, the initial and terminal points are the same (since the path is closed and is traversed only once). Therefore, ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$ and we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "60ff33e4-fe39-587a-9f58-4a9d2690c97f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426491}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2271c2f3-6a0e-52db-a073-7c6e91fd8bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257064} {:answer/id #uuid "7a614516-aa62-5836-8581-97092175214f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257865} {:answer/id #uuid "7f262654-5960-5b84-bd60-37392d856cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257916} {:answer/id #uuid "f2324693-6303-570a-a9ea-853d9ea6b405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258889} {:answer/id #uuid "f35b33e2-0c6e-55fd-902a-35519905732a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257916} :answer-field/id #uuid "a181e6a4-7e9b-5c76-a47e-43a0425e674b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5026f273-e6ca-5d94-86d8-dd1bd2a406be" :question/math-academy-id "q-124281" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b095a29b433107c37cd7e28c853d9d3d3547fb64a9e874783206b86c0aca6c1.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(1,-3)$ to the point $(4,0),$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=4$ and ${x}_{1}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=4-1=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6fc8bd4b-f8e0-533e-a614-47a2e4b20930" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1832c026-532a-5b68-9cf9-1e99d898f97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426477} {:answer/id #uuid "77030057-915a-5bf2-b853-884b00d3a0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426482} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fa88c2-055c-5698-ac15-9fab33508e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426483} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2086cd-91f8-5e89-a8cf-09b5f2f54c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426484} {:answer/id #uuid "c85b8a30-49c9-5708-b7f3-202e64369872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426482} :answer-field/id #uuid "39828f92-e829-5bba-b1e4-9d6adfcd1f10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6d2ac1d-75ed-5453-8dc7-92123acc1f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-124308" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb482ddbb5bde83cfd6ed383b0f321cb27377f34aafcb9e74e44d91824dcf366.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is the path comprising of a line segment and a semicircle of radius $1,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=-3$ and ${x}_{1}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=(-3)-2=-5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c27ffad8-d3ca-5837-bf61-af8fcb5c79a8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426492}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426475}]}
